  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 344 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA cold front situated across the Sandhills Saturday morning, \nwill be forced south into southern Nebraska by Saturday \nafternoon. At the same time, strong mid level frontogenesis will\nenter the northern panhandle Saturday morning, spreading to the\neast southeast into the afternoon hours. With this evenings \nforecast initialization, likely (60 to 70%) and categorical \n(80%+) pops were added to a large portion of the Sandhills into \nportions of central Nebraska and the I-80 corridor east of \nSutherland for Saturday. Looking at the NBM ensemble forecast \ntonight, there is a 60-70% chance of QPF reaching a tenth of an \ninch (12 hr ending at midnight Saturday evening) along a \ncorridor from Alliance to Broken Bow. When this threshold is \nraised to a quarter inch, there is a 20-30% chance along this \nsame corridor. That being said, there is decent confidence in \nlocations reaching a tenth of an inch of QPF with this system \nand high confidence in at least reaching measurable (0.01\") of \nQPF. With this in mind, along with good support from the 00z \nGFS,12z and 18z EC solns, widespread likely and categorical pops\nwill be retained. With respect to snow potential with this \nsystem Saturday. Current forecast trends have readings in the \nmiddle to upper 30s Saturday along the precipitation axis and \nforecast soundings from the GFS are indicative of all rain \n(except the northwestern forecast area). A quick look at snow \nprobabilities in the NBM ensemble forecast has a around a 50% \nchance for 0.5\" of 24 hr snowfall over Sheridan into far \nnorthern Garden, northwestern Grant and southwestern Cherry \nCounty (ending at 6 PM CT Saturday). However, the latest \ndeterministic GFS tonight and 18z EC solns accumulate anywhere \nfrom 1 to locally 3 inches of snow along a corridor from \nsouthern Sheridan into Custer County by Saturday evening. These \ndifferences between the NBM and deterministic solutions are \nconcerning and problematic for the snow forecast Saturday as the\nbulk of precipitation with this system appears to fall during \ndaylight hours where temps are the warmest. This lends support \nfor a predominately rain/snow mix and little to no snow \naccumulations. However, if temperatures trend lower with \nsubsequent model runs and mid level forcing remains in place, \nthe threat for accumulating snows will increase Saturday. For \nnow, will utilize the blended forecast, but highlight the \nuncertainty for accumulating snow with the HWO and social media \nposts. The precipitation threat will shift quickly east of the \narea Saturday evening. Behind the exiting system, chilly air \nwill remain in place, mainly over the eastern half of the \nforecast area Sunday. Highs will struggle to get out of the \nlower 40s across the area. Low amplitude ridging, followed by \nzonal, then southwesterly flow aloft, will lead to warmer and \ndry conditions for Monday through Wednesday. Highs Monday and \nTuesday will range from 45 to 55 degrees with widespread 50s for\nWednesday. An upper level system will track from the Four \nCorners onto the central and southern plains late next week. \nCurrent forecast trends have this un-phased with the northern \nstream so the bulk of precipitation with this system appears to \nbe all liquid ATTM. .",
